==English==

===Etymology===
1820s, from earlier German use; earlier [[hylical]] (1708). From {{der|en|grc|ῡ̔́λη||matter}} {{suffix|en||ic|gloss2=of or pertaining to}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhaɪlɪk/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Having to do with, or of the [[nature]] of, [[matter]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1828|chapter=a part of mankind were by original constitution altogether ''hylic'' or material|title=The New Jerusalem magazine and theological inspector|page=155}}
#*'''1840''' "three orders of beings,&mdash;the spiritual, physical and hylic natures."  Johann Joseph Ignaz von Döllinger (trans. Edward Cox), ''A History of the Church'', Volume 1 (1840), p. 133.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|hyletic}}
* {{l|en|material}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|hylism}}
* {{l|en|hylicism}}
* {{l|en|hylicist}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|gnosticism}} The [[basest]] type of [[man]] in the [[gnostic]] [[theologian]] [[Valentinus]]' [[triadic]] grouping; a person focused on neither [[intellectual]] ([[psychic]]) nor [[spiritual]] ([[pneumatic]]) reality.

